# cliProxy Core Context

- `cliProxy` is an OpenAI-compatible multi-provider AI proxy for CLI model clients (Codex CLI, Copilot CLI, etc.).
- Supports DeepSeek, Kimi, MiniMax, and Azure Foundry providers.
- `api/proxy.js` is the main entrypoint and holds the provider orchestration.
- `api/models.js` handles model parsing, public/private model IDs, and provider routing.
- Azure adapters live in `api/azure-openai.js` and `api/azure-anthropic.js`.
- Reasoning and cache helpers live in `api/reasoning.js`, `api/cache.js`, and `api/kv.js`.
- All `api/*.js` files are shared edge-safe modules. Do not introduce `node:*` imports or `ioredis` there.
- `server.js` is the Docker-only wrapper around the shared proxy logic.
- `vercel.json` and `cloud-functions/` provide platform routing entrypoints.
- `cursorProxy` at `/home/opc/cursorProxy` is the sibling reference implementation from which cliProxy was inherited. Some rules and architectural docs may reference it.
- Manual verification prompts live under `.cursor/test-cases/`.
- `wiki/` is a separate git clone for the GitHub wiki (`git@github.com:lqdflying/cliProxy.wiki.git`). Do not commit wiki changes in the main repo.
- **Release process**: "tag and release" means TWO steps — (1) `GIT_COMMITTER_DATE="<commit-date>" git tag -a vX.Y.Z <commit> -m "vX.Y.Z"` to create an annotated tag with NO description body, using the commit's date as tagger date so tags sort chronologically on GitHub, then (2) `gh release create vX.Y.Z --notes "..."` with the full changelog/description. Tags must never carry descriptions; descriptions belong only on GitHub Releases. Push the tag and create the release in the same turn.
